  <data key="d7">[{"pat_id": "US-5919002-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the transfer of the water entering at the upstream catch basin to the one downstream with little or no leakage is thus assured"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "the difference in the detected rates of temperature change during the wait period 695 is explained by the position of the first temperature sensor 414 being located downstream of the condensate management portion 400 (i.e., the drain pipe 394) such that the leaking water is inhibited from entering the drain line 398 to be detected by the first temperature sensor 414"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "in many situations these changes in pressure upstream of the devices while there being no flow detected by the flow sensors would be indicative of a leak upstream of the buildings"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "specific embodiments relate to directing a fluid stream through an outlet at an outer surface of the pipeline to cause a vibration in the pipeline which simulates a leak in the pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the impact of the fluid stream on the outer surface of the pipeline 4 causes a vibration in the pipeline which mimics the vibration caused by a leak in the pipeline 4"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in any case, the vibration caused by the fluid stream mimics the vibration caused by a leak in the pipeline 4"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "therefore, the thermal change in the pipeline 4 generated by directing a fluid stream onto the pipeline 4 can be used to simulate the thermal change caused by a leak in the pipeline 4"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in an embodiment, therefore, the system 2 can be used to selectively direct a fluid stream at the outer surface of the pipeline 4 to cause a vibration and/or a thermal change in the pipeline 4 which mimics the vibration and/or the thermal change produced by a leak in the pipeline 4"}, {"pat_id": "US-10704740-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "therefore, the vibration caused by the fluid stream may more accurately mimic the vibration caused by the substance leaking from the pipeline"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3374747-B1", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "in many embodiments, leak detection technique m4 in addition to m1 can detect leaks by monitoring a pressure stream time domain signal detected by pressure sensor 226 (fig"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "the difference in the detected rates of temperature change during the wait period 695 is explained by the position of the first temperature sensor 414 being located downstream of the condensate management portion 400 (i.e., the drain pipe 394) such that the leaking water is inhibit (determine)ed from entering the drain line 398 to be detected by the first temperature sensor 414"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "in many situations these changes in pressure upstream of the devices while (determine) there being no flow detected by the flow sensors would be indicative of a leak upstream of the buildings"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "9c, background noise is filtered from the sensor signal(s) to determine building movement, detect upstream leaks and possibly take remedial action in the depicted process 900-3"}, {"pat_id": "US-11629721-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "a continuous small leak (or a major leak), for example, can prevent (therefore) the tank from refill and continue water stream until it is fixed"}, {"pat_id": "US-6627073-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "if the water level in the area on the uppermost stream side in the electrolytic tank is controlled in a predetermined range, as in the claim 16, therefore, it is possible to more reliably prevent the water from leaking from the electrolytic tank"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "when detecting upstream leaks, the cloud analyzer 108 can activate a shut off valve for the plumbing system 116 to determine if the leak is within the plumbing system 116 or outside the building 112 or unit"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "the shutoff valve 532 can be used to prevent (determine) flooding for leaks downstream of the water device 120"}, {"pat_id": "US-6543479-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "if the leak is of a sufficient magnitude, such as a steady stream, the timer will become (determine) activated and continue through its cycle to close off valve 10 and stop all water flow"}, {"pat_id": "US-11905688-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "by recognizing a leak from multiple locations of the plumbing system 116, it can be determined if the leak is inside the building 112 or upstream from the building 112 looking back into the water main"}, {"pat_id": "US-10410501-B2", "para_id": "para_6", "text": "the water meter and leak detection system 10, 126, 200 is configured to determine, based on an analysis of the stream of data points, whether a leak exists in at least one of the water fixtures"}, {"pat_id": "US-11095960-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "the water meter and leak detection system is configured to determine, based on an analysis of the stream of data points, whether a leak exists in at least one of the water fixtures"}]</data>
