  <data key="d7">[{"pat_id": "US-7510656-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "submerged combustion heaters, or other heat sources, may be used to raise water temperature as desired, such as in the winter to prevent water freezing if the aerator 106 is an outdoor pond"}, {"pat_id": "US-7527736-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "submerged combustion heaters, or other heat sources, may be used to raise water temperature as desired, such as in the winter to prevent water freezing if the aerator 106 is an outdoor pond"}, {"pat_id": "US-8105488-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "submerged combustion heaters, or other heat sources, may be used to raise water temperature as desired, such as in the winter to prevent water freezing if the aerator 106 is an outdoor pond"}, {"pat_id": "KR-100871326-B1", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "in order to prevent the water of the sedimentation basin 10 from freezing in the heat generating tube 50, the temperature is again sent to the recovery distributor 67 by the recovery connecting tube 54-1, and the heat is again transferred from the heat exchanger 64"}, {"pat_id": "US-4672990-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "despite colder above-ground ambient temperatures, radiant heat from main water supply line 2 should prevent freezing of main branch line 3 and main valve 4"}, {"pat_id": "US-10981818-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the higher wattage and overcapacity of the heating element is preferred to ensure that the chamber temperature is maintained because (thereby) the air pump would bring in cold air in very cold or freezing weather"}, {"pat_id": "US-4236382-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in any of the vacuum freezing processes described, an aqueous solution is introduced into a chamber which is maintained at a pressure that is somewhat lower than the vapor pressure of the solution at the freezing temperature of the solution to thereby simultaneously flash vaporize water and form ice crystals"}, {"pat_id": "US-5059228-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "referring to the processing of an aqueous solution by any vacuum freezing process, the aqueous solution is introduced into a chamber which is maintained at a pressure that is somewhat lower than the vapor pressure of the solution at the freezing temperature of the solution to thereby simultaneously flash vaporize water and form ice crystals"}, {"pat_id": "US-5080704-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "referring to the processing of an aqueous solution by any vacuum freezing process, the aqueous solution is introduced into a chamber which is maintained at a pressure that is somewhat lower than the vapor pressure of the solution at the freezing temperature of the solution to thereby simultaneously flash vaporize water and form ice crystals"}, {"pat_id": "US-5448892-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "vacuum freezing processes referring to the processing of an aqueous solution by any vacuum freezing process, the aqueous solution is introduced into a chamber which is maintained at a pressure that is somewhat lower than the vapor pressure of the solution at the freezing temperature of the solution to thereby simultaneously flash vaporize water and form ice crystals"}, {"pat_id": "US-5553637-A", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the second chamber 31 has a primary inlet 34 connected at 35 to the outlet 11 via (thereby) the supply line section 13a subjected to freezing temperature, i.e., aboveground"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101244452-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "when the heater 610 of the heater unit 600 is disposed in the conduit 510 as in one embodiment of the present invention, the heater 610 is directly in contact with the fire water in the conduit 510, and the heater 610 can directly heat the fire water in the conduit 510, the heating efficiency and energy utilization efficiency of the fire water in the conduit 510 is excellent, thereby freezing the fire water in the conduit 510 quickly it can be heated to the above temperature"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101244452-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the heater unit 600 and the water temperature detection unit 700 are both electrically connected to the control unit 800, and when the temperature of the fire water in the pipeline 510 sensed by the water temperature detection unit 700 is below the freezing point, the control unit 800 ) provides power to the heater unit 600 to directly supply heat to the fire hydrant 540 and the fire fighting water in the conduit 510 to prevent freezing due to (thereby) freezing of the conduit 510"}, {"pat_id": "US-11608618-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in addition, the primary temperature sensor package 702 and the secondary temperature sensor package 706 may be utilized to raise the temperature of the fluid to prevent (thereby) the fluid within the fluid conduit system 720 from freezing"}, {"pat_id": "US-11608618-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "thus, a temperature or temperature range may be chosen at which the microprocessor 712 will add heat energy to the fluid in the fluid conduit system 720 before the water reaches 32 degrees fahrenheit to prevent (thereby) it from freezing"}, {"pat_id": "US-11608618-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in addition, the primary temperature sensor package 806 and the secondary temperature sensor package 840 may be utilized to raise the temperature of the fluid to prevent (thereby) the fluid within the fluid conduit system 810 from freezing"}, {"pat_id": "US-11608618-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the system 1100 is configured for use on a fluid conduit system 1102 located within a structure 1101 to prevent (thereby) the fluid conduit system 1102 from damage due to freezing fluid upon detection of freezing conditions, such as the temperature of the fluid in the fluid conduit system 1102 and the temperature of the environment surrounding the fluid conduit system 1102"}, {"pat_id": "US-11608618-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "by placing the primary heating element 1120 and secondary heating element 1122