  <data key="d4">["cause", "increase"]</data>
  <data key="d5">["attribute", "vegetation", "type", "system", "information", "intrusion", "amount", "content", "water", "area", "growth", "imagery", "damage", "motion", "vicinity", "terrain", "location", "vhr", "monitoring", "step", "leakage", "index", "tree", "factor", "part", "combination", "estimate", "methane", "increase", "plant", "cause", "root", "risk", "network", "pipeline", "satellite", "leak", "soil"]</data>
  <data key="d6">["cause", "lead", "contribute", "generate", "be", "compare", "detect", "impact", "contain", "outline", "base", "involve", "increase", "relate"]</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"pat_id": "US-6243483-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the infrared information contained within vhr satellite imagery is potentially useful for detecting methane related vegetation damage, which can be caused by pipeline leakage"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "and generating, by the water network monitoring (increase) system, a leakage risk index based at least in part on the estimate of vegetation intrusion into the water network and the monitoring of the amount of terrain motion around the water network"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "increase in water content in the vicinity of a water network leak may lead to an increase in vegetation growth in that area compared to other nearby areas"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "one of the principal causes of water network leakage is vegetation intrusion, where roots from plants or trees physically impact on the water network, causing damage and ultimately a leak"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "step 1309 involves generating, by the water network monitoring (increase) system, a leakage risk index based at least in part on the estimate of vegetation intrusion into the water network and the monitoring of the amount of terrain motion around the water network"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "as outlined above, vegetation intrusion and terrain motion are the main environmental causes of water network leakage"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3655743-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "the combination of the physical attributes of the soil and the physical attributes for the type of the classified vegetation at each location, are all factors that may contribute to (increase) vegetation intrusion that can cause a water network leak"}]</data>
</edge>
