ure sensor 6066 (see, e.g., fig"}, {"pat_id": "US-11325845-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "in block 7528, the controller 6034 may receive (thus) data from each of the product temperature sensors (e.g"}, {"pat_id": "US-11325845-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "in block 7832, the controller 6034 may find a derivate of the product level based on data receive (thus)d from the product temperature sensor(s) 6082a-d"}, {"pat_id": "US-11325845-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "in some embodiments, the individual derivatives determined from each product temperature sensor 6082a-d may all be required to be less than the threshold in order for the controller 6034 to proceed to block 7840"}, {"pat_id": "US-11325845-B2", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "in some embodiments, if any of the individual derivatives determined from each product temperature sensor 6082a-d are less than the threshold, the controller 6034 may proceed to block 7844"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "if, in block 7348, the difference for each is greater than a threshold set for each respective parameter, the controller 6034 calculates a derivative based on data receive (thus)d from at least one temperature sensor in the system 6000 in block 7351"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "for example, the controller 6034 may calculate a derivative based on data receive (thus)d from a low pressure steam temperature sensor 6066 in block 7351"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "otherwise, the controller 6034 may, in block 7392, receive (thus) temperature data signals from one or more product temperature sensor (e.g"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "if, at block 7069, the system 6000 is currently in a normal water production mode, the target temperature 7068 along with a product or condensate output temperature 7070 determined from data provided by a product output sensor (e.g"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "a current low pressure steam temperature may be receive (thus)d by the controller 6034 in block 6884 from, for example, a low pressure steam temperature sensor 6066 (see, e.g., fig"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "in block 7528, the controller 6034 may receive (thus) data from each of the product temperature sensors (e.g"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "in block 7832, the controller 6034 may find a derivate of the product level based on data receive (thus)d from the product temperature sensor(s) 6082a-d"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "in some embodiments, the individual derivatives determined from each product temperature sensor 6082a-d may all be required to be less than the threshold in order for the controller 6034 to proceed to block 7840"}, {"pat_id": "US-11352267-B2", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "in some embodiments, if any of the individual derivatives determined from each product temperature sensor 6082a-d are less than the threshold, the controller 6034 may proceed to block 7844"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "claim_8", "text": "optionally wherein the drain blockage is determined by the controller (142) when the temperature reading of the first temperature sensor (414) is about the same as the temperature reading of the second temperature sensor (418)"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "the controller 142 is further configured to compare the temperature readings of the first, second, and third temperature sensors 114, 118, 122 to determine whether an error has occurred in the water heater 10 (e.g., leakage, drain blockage), as further discussed below"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "for example, when determining a drain blockage, the controller 142 may be configured to query the first and second temperature sensors 114, 118 during morning hours when it is most likely the user will require (determine) use of hot water"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "alternatively, the temperature readings of the first temperature sensor 414 during the heating mode may also be used to determine a drain blockage"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3779320-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "in particular, when the temperature readings of the first temperature sensor 414 decrease toward the minimum temperature limit 682 during the heating mode, the controller will determine that there is a drain blockage"}, {"pat_id": "US-11761838-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "when the temperature distribution in equipment such as pumps, rotors, compressors and turbines changes or when the temperature ranges exceed normal values, the nanoparticles based sensors can alert and prevent breakage and down time of the asset"}, {"pat_id": "US-11066813-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in some example embodiments, the temperature sensor (115) may be used to prevent pipes from freezing, and the pressure sensor (115) may be used to prevent pipes from bursting"}, {"pat_id": "KR-100901209-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "on the other hand, the water supply control device 250 by checking the pressure in the water supply pipe 230 through the pressure sensor unit 260, it is possible to prevent the water from flowing into the water supply pipe 230 is frozen or blocked due to low temperature, the pipe is broken"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101327389-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "that is, by checking the condensate and curing temperature throughout the construction by the moisture sensor and the temperature sensor, it is possible to contribute to the quality improvement by checking the uniform curing"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101147332-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "water pollution monitoring system according to the prior art, by providing a temperature control device inside the water pollution monitoring system so that the sensitivity of the sensor is not affected by the external temperature, and also provides a sensor cleaning device inside the sensor to improve the sensitivity of the sensor it s about making sure you re not affected by contaminants"}, {"pat_id": "US-11892393-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "for example, as noted above, the rate of deposit of lead oxide on the contaminant sensor 175 is dependent on ph, temperature, conductivity, and ionic strength"}, {"pat_id": "US-6315886-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in addition, the invention contemplates in-line monitoring with sensors and microprocessors for automatic computer-assisted process control, such as ph sensors, uv and visible light, sensors for biological contaminants, temperature, etc"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3032245-B1", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "accordingly, whether the impedance response of interest is due to (increase) the liquid property of the circulating liquid and the electrode surface coating or due to the change in temperature is required to be known, which would otherwise provide a concern of malfunction of the anticorrosion performance degradation detection sensor 10"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3032245-B1", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "the impedance of the circulating liquid and the surface of the detection electrode 1 measured by the anticorrosion performance degradation detection sensor 10 increases by 2% when the temperature of the circulating liquid decreases by 1 c"}, {"pat_id": "US-10722824-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in second (sensor) compartment 23 to determine the temperature at the ph and corrosion sensors and permit correction of their readings"}, {"pat_id": "US-10722824-B2", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "in the second compartment, a corrosion monitor, ph meter, and temperature sensor were installed for continuous monitoring (increase)"}, {"pat_id": "US-12163944-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "temperature sensors traditionally used within the industry are of resistive temperature detector (rtd) type, and are typically configured with a stainless steel dome to prevent malfunction due to (increase) corrosion and water ingress issues"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101521944-B1", "para_id": "claim_12", "text": "a heating control method for a steam cracking device, a step of applying a heater power to the steam heating element when the steam generating switch is turned on while the power switch is turned on and measuring the temperature of the steam heating element through the steam heating element temperature sensor"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3706066-B1", "para_id": "para_16", "text": "in another embodiment, not covered by the claimed invention, moreover the pressure sensor comprises another bragg grating, installed on a part of said sensor not deforming after pressure, such for example a cylindrical body, so that it is subject only to deformation due to (therefore) temperature"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3706066-B1", "para_id": "para_18", "text": "preferably, in addition the pressure sensor comprises another bragg grating, installed on a part of said sensor not deforming after liquid flow, so that it is subject only to deformation due to (therefore) temperature"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101967405-B1", "para_id": "para_4", "text": "therefore, if a point sensor is used to measure the temperature and strain in the water pipe, it is not possible to confirm the position of the sensor even if leakage or deformation occurs in the interval between the sensors"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3032245-B1", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "accordingly, whether the impedance response of interest is due to (increase) the liquid property of the circulating liquid and the electrode surface coating or due to the change in temperature is required to be known, which would otherwise provide a concern of malfunction of the anticorrosion performance degradation detection sensor 10"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3032245-B1", "para_id": "para_5", "text": "the impedance of the circulating liquid and the surface of the detection electrode 1 measured by the anticorrosion performance degradation detection sensor 10 increases by 2% when the temperature of the circulating liquid decreases by 1 c"}, {"pat_id": "US-11892393-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "for example, as noted above, the rate of deposit of lead oxide on the contaminant sensor 175 is dependent on ph, temperature, conductivity, and ionic strength"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101090211-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "in addition, the display unit 50 displays the temperature of the water receive (affect)d from the temperature sensor 14, the ph concentration of the electrolysis tank 40, the time, etc., the user at a glance using the electrolysis according to the present invention (1) outputs the status of)"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101262046-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "various sensors such as temperature can be installed to detect the change of concentration of sterilization ion efficiently and actively.in order to efficiently generate concentration of sterilization ion through electrolysis when water passes, the electrode affects the flow rate of water"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101486499-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the first sensor unit 30 is connected to the filter and the electrolysis unit 10, (10) receive (affect)s the ballast water that has been filtered and electrolyzed, and measures the conductivity, temperature, and total residual oxidant (tro) concentration of the ship ballast water"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101486501-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the first sensor unit 30 is connected to the filter and the electrolysis unit 10, (10) receive (affect)s the ballast water that has been filtered and electrolyzed, and measures the conductivity, temperature, and total residual oxidant (tro) concentration of the ship ballast water"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101486502-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the first sensor unit 30 is connected to the filter and the electrolysis unit 10, (10) receive (affect)s the ballast water that has been filtered and electrolyzed, and measures the conductivity, temperature, and total residual oxidant (tro) concentration of the ship ballast water"}, {"pat_id": "KR-101486503-B1", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the first sensor unit 30 is connected to the filter and the electrolysis unit 10, (10) receive (affect)s the ballast water that has been filtered and electrolyzed, and measures the conductivity, temperature, and total residual oxidant (tro) concentration of the ship ballast water"}, {"pat_id": "KR-102578604-B1", "para_id": "para_2", "text": "the first, second, and third coolant pump control units (240, 250, and 260) receive (affect) electrolysis reactor temperature data measured from the electrolysis reactor temperature sensor 140, and when the electrolysis reactor temperature is higher than the set temperature, the first coolant pump (p1), serves to operate the second coolant pump (p2) and the third coolant pump (p3)"}, {"pat_id": "US-10087095-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "the first sensor unit (30) is connected with the filter and electrolysis unit (10), receive (affect)s the treated ballast water after the process of the filtering and electrolysis, and measures conductivity, temperature, and tro (total residual oxidant) concentration of the received ballast water"}, {"pat_id": "US-11014837-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "furthermore, the monitoring (affect) unit 360 may monitor a temperature, degree of electrolysis and/or water level of the sea or river, which is measured using an sbe37im sensor"}, {"pat_id": "US-10753900-B2", "para_id": "para_1", "text": "because the resistance of meas can directly demonstrate the electrode fouling status, the resistance of each mea sensors was used as an indicator of the whole mea and measured at ambient temperature (25 c.) using a digital multimeter weekly for 4 weeks to elucidate the electrode fouling influence"}, {"pat_id": "EP-3769176-B1", "para_id": "para_3", "text": "temperature sensor 38 is used to collect real-time temperature data and monitor the temperature of the incoming water to determine if condit